# The kings and princes of Wales, batch 3 (November 2026): the women of the royal houses, and the last heir of Gwynedd.
# Siwan, Gwenllian ferch Gruffudd, Gwenllian ferch Llywelyn, Owain Lawgoch.
# Same format and house rules as src/princes.py, which adds LONG and CITES from here to its own.
# Sources checked (November 2026): the Dictionary of Welsh Biography on Joan (Siwan) and on Owain ap Thomas ap Rhodri;
# Gerald of Wales, Itinerary through Wales, Book I chapter 9 (tr. Sir Richard Colt Hoare); Brut y Tywysogion
# (tr. Thomas Jones, 1952); Wikipedia on Gwenllian ferch Gruffydd, Gwenllian of Wales and Owain Lawgoch.
# Not confirmed, so left out: the year of the Sempringham memorial stone; the exact words of any war cry for Gwenllian.

LONG = {}

LONG["siwan"] = (
 ["Siwan, Joan to the English, was the daughter of King John of England and the wife of Llywelyn Fawr, and for more than thirty years she was the voice of Gwynedd at the English court. Again and again, when war threatened between her father, then her brother, and her husband, it was Siwan who crossed the border to make the peace. She was styled Lady of Wales, and she was the mother of Dafydd ap Llywelyn, the first ruler of Wales to call himself Prince of Wales.",
  "|| Born | about 1191, perhaps in Normandy",
  "|| Father | King John of England",
  "|| Married | Llywelyn ab Iorwerth, Llywelyn Fawr, about 1205",
  "|| Title | Lady of North Wales; from 1230 Lady of Wales",
  "|| Children | Dafydd ap Llywelyn, Elen, Susanna, and probably Gwladus Ddu and others",
  "|| Died | 2 February 1237, at the royal court of Abergwyngregyn",
  "|| Buried | Llanfaes, Ynys Môn, where Llywelyn founded a friary in her memory",
  "## A king’s daughter for Gwynedd",
  "Siwan was the only known daughter of King John born outside his marriage. The first glimpse of her in the records may be in December 1203, when “the king’s daughter” was brought over from Normandy at the king’s cost. John had a match in mind. Llywelyn ab Iorwerth had made himself master of Gwynedd, and John wanted him as an ally. By October 1204 Siwan was betrothed to Llywelyn, and in April 1205 the king granted Ellesmere to Llywelyn as her marriage portion. They were married about that time, and Siwan came to the courts of Gwynedd at Aber and Aberffraw.[[cite:dwb-siwan,wiki-siwan]]",
  "## The peacemaker of 1211",
  "Peace between her father and her husband did not last. In 1211 John came into Gwynedd with a great army and pressed Llywelyn hard. Then, in the words of the Welsh chronicle, Llywelyn “sent his wife, the king’s daughter, to him” to make peace. Siwan went to her father and came back with terms. Llywelyn kept his crown, and Gwynedd lived to rise again. The next summer, when John gathered an army at Nottingham to invade Wales once more, a letter from Siwan helped persuade him to turn back.[[cite:dwb-siwan,brut-jones]]",
  "> sent his wife, the king’s daughter, to him to seek to make peace with the king | Brut y Tywysogion, 1211",
  "## The voice of Wales at the English court",
  "For the rest of her life Siwan was Llywelyn’s chief envoy. In 1214 and 1215 she won the release of five of his men held by the crown. In 1224 she was given safe conduct to Worcester to prepare a peace conference with her half-brother, King Henry III. In 1226 she met the king at Shrewsbury with Llywelyn and their son Dafydd, and in 1228 she went to Shrewsbury alone and arranged a truce herself. That October she stood at Westminster as Llywelyn’s ambassador when Dafydd did homage to the king. In 1232 she met Henry three times at Shrewsbury on Llywelyn’s behalf, and in one letter to her brother she wrote that she was “grieved beyond measure” that peace had broken down.[[cite:dwb-siwan]]",
  "In 1226, with Henry’s support, the Pope declared Siwan legitimate. It raised her standing and that of her children, and it strengthened the claim of her son Dafydd to follow his father as the one heir of Gwynedd.[[cite:dwb-siwan,wiki-siwan]]",
  "## 1230",
  "At Easter 1230 the Marcher lord William de Braose, held at Llywelyn’s court for ransom, was found in Llywelyn’s chamber with Siwan, and Llywelyn had him hanged. Siwan was kept apart for about a year. Then Llywelyn brought her back to his side, and from that time she was called Lady of Wales, and she went on as his envoy as before. Saunders Lewis made the story into one of the great plays of the Welsh language, ‘Siwan’.[[cite:dwb-siwan,wiki-siwan]]",
  "## Llanfaes",
  "Siwan died at Abergwyngregyn on 2 February 1237. Her body was carried over the Menai Strait to Ynys Môn and buried at Llanfaes, and Llywelyn, in his grief, founded a house of Franciscan friars there in her memory. When the friary was pulled down under Henry VIII, a stone coffin was taken from Llanfaes. It now lies in the church of St Mary and St Nicholas in Beaumaris, with a carved figure of a crowned lady on its lid, and it has long been held to be hers.[[cite:dwb-siwan,wiki-siwan]]"],
 ["Siwan, Joan i’r Saeson, oedd merch y Brenin John o Loegr a gwraig Llywelyn Fawr, ac am fwy na deng mlynedd ar hugain hi oedd llais Gwynedd yn llys Lloegr. Dro ar ôl tro, pan fygythiai rhyfel rhwng ei thad, wedyn ei brawd, a’i gŵr, Siwan a groesai’r ffin i wneud heddwch. Fe’i gelwid yn Arglwyddes Cymru, a hi oedd mam Dafydd ap Llywelyn, y rheolwr cyntaf yng Nghymru i’w alw ei hun yn Dywysog Cymru.",
  "|| Ganed | tua 1191, efallai yn Normandi",
  "|| Tad | y Brenin John o Loegr",
  "|| Priododd | Llywelyn ab Iorwerth, Llywelyn Fawr, tua 1205",
  "|| Teitl | Arglwyddes Gogledd Cymru; o 1230 Arglwyddes Cymru",
  "|| Plant | Dafydd ap Llywelyn, Elen, Susanna, ac yn ôl pob tebyg Gwladus Ddu ac eraill",
  "|| Bu farw | 2 Chwefror 1237, yn llys brenhinol Abergwyngregyn",
  "|| Claddwyd | Llan-faes, Ynys Môn, lle sefydlodd Llywelyn frodordy er cof amdani",
  "## Merch brenin i Wynedd",
  "Siwan oedd yr unig ferch hysbys i’r Brenin John a aned y tu allan i’w briodas. Efallai mai’r cip cyntaf arni yn y cofnodion yw ym mis Rhagfyr 1203, pan ddygwyd “merch y brenin” drosodd o Normandi ar draul y brenin. Roedd gan John briodas mewn golwg. Roedd Llywelyn ab Iorwerth wedi’i wneud ei hun yn feistr ar Wynedd, ac roedd John am ei gael yn gynghreiriad. Erbyn Hydref 1204 roedd Siwan wedi’i dyweddïo â Llywelyn, ac ym mis Ebrill 1205 rhoddodd y brenin Ellesmere i Lywelyn yn waddol iddi. Priodwyd hwy tua’r adeg honno, a daeth Siwan i lysoedd Gwynedd yn Aber ac Aberffraw.[[cite:dwb-siwan,wiki-siwan]]",
  "## Heddychwraig 1211",
  "Ni pharhaodd yr heddwch rhwng ei thad a’i gŵr. Yn 1211 daeth John i Wynedd gyda byddin fawr a gwasgu’n galed ar Lywelyn. Yna, yng ngeiriau’r cronicl Cymraeg, anfonodd Llywelyn “ei wraig, merch y brenin, ato” i wneud heddwch. Aeth Siwan at ei thad a dychwelyd ag amodau. Cadwodd Llywelyn ei goron, a bu Gwynedd fyw i godi eto. Yr haf canlynol, pan gasglodd John fyddin yn Nottingham i oresgyn Cymru unwaith eto, helpodd llythyr gan Siwan i’w berswadio i droi’n ôl.[[cite:dwb-siwan,brut-jones]]",
  "> anfonodd ei wraig, merch y brenin, ato i geisio gwneud heddwch â’r brenin | Brut y Tywysogion, 1211",
  "## Llais Cymru yn llys Lloegr",
  "Am weddill ei hoes Siwan oedd prif gennad Llywelyn. Yn 1214 a 1215 enillodd ryddid i bump o’i wŷr a ddelid gan y goron. Yn 1224 cafodd hawl i deithio’n ddiogel i Gaerwrangon i baratoi cynhadledd heddwch â’i hanner brawd, y Brenin Harri III. Yn 1226 cyfarfu â’r brenin yn Amwythig gyda Llywelyn a’u mab Dafydd, ac yn 1228 aeth i Amwythig ar ei phen ei hun a threfnu cadoediad ei hunan. Y mis Hydref hwnnw safodd yn Westminster yn llysgennad Llywelyn pan dalodd Dafydd wrogaeth i’r brenin. Yn 1232 cyfarfu â Harri dair gwaith yn Amwythig ar ran Llywelyn, ac mewn un llythyr at ei brawd ysgrifennodd ei bod “yn drist y tu hwnt i fesur” fod yr heddwch wedi chwalu.[[cite:dwb-siwan]]",
  "Yn 1226, gyda chefnogaeth Harri, cyhoeddodd y Pab fod Siwan yn gyfreithlon. Cododd hynny ei statws hi a statws ei phlant, a chryfhaodd hawl ei mab Dafydd i ddilyn ei dad yn unig etifedd Gwynedd.[[cite:dwb-siwan,wiki-siwan]]",
  "## 1230",
  "Adeg y Pasg 1230 cafwyd yr arglwydd o’r Mers Gwilym Brewys, a gedwid yn llys Llywelyn am bridwerth, yn ystafell Llywelyn gyda Siwan, a chrogwyd ef ar orchymyn Llywelyn. Cadwyd Siwan ar wahân am tua blwyddyn. Yna daeth Llywelyn â hi’n ôl i’w ochr, ac o’r adeg honno fe’i gelwid yn Arglwyddes Cymru, ac aeth ymlaen yn gennad iddo fel cynt. Gwnaeth Saunders Lewis y stori yn un o ddramâu mawr yr iaith Gymraeg, ‘Siwan’.[[cite:dwb-siwan,wiki-siwan]]",
  "## Llan-faes",
  "Bu farw Siwan yn Abergwyngregyn ar 2 Chwefror 1237. Cariwyd ei chorff dros Afon Menai i Ynys Môn a’i gladdu yn Llan-faes, ac yn ei alar sefydlodd Llywelyn dŷ o frodyr Ffransisgaidd yno er cof amdani. Pan dynnwyd y brodordy i lawr dan Harri VIII, cymerwyd arch garreg o Lan-faes. Mae hi bellach yn eglwys y Santes Fair a Sant Nicolas ym Miwmares, gyda delw o arglwyddes goronog ar ei chaead, a chredwyd ers tro mai ei harch hi yw hi.[[cite:dwb-siwan,wiki-siwan]]"],
)

LONG["gwenllian"] = (
 ["Gwenllian ferch Gruffudd was a princess of Gwynedd and of Deheubarth, and in 1136 she rode at the head of an army against the Normans. She was the daughter of Gruffudd ap Cynan, the sister of Owain Gwynedd, and the mother of the Lord Rhys. She fell in battle near Kidwelly, and her death set the whole of south and west Wales alight. Gerald of Wales, writing half a century later, compared her to the queen of the Amazons.",
  "|| Father | Gruffudd ap Cynan, king of Gwynedd",
  "|| Mother | Angharad ferch Owain",
  "|| Brothers | Owain Gwynedd and Cadwaladr",
  "|| Married | Gruffudd ap Rhys, prince of Deheubarth, soon after 1116",
  "|| Sons | Morgan, Maelgwn, Maredudd, Rhys (the Lord Rhys) and others",
  "|| Fell | 1136, at Maes Gwenllian near Kidwelly",
  "## Daughter of Gwynedd",
  "Gwenllian grew up at the court of her father Gruffudd ap Cynan, who had won back Gwynedd from the Normans. Around 1113 a young prince of the south came north: Gruffudd ap Rhys, the son of Rhys ap Tewdwr, last king of Deheubarth, and the brother of Nest. He and Gwenllian fell in love and went away together, and they were married soon after 1116. She shared his years in the hills of Ystrad Tywi as he fought to win back his father’s kingdom, and she bore him many children, among them Rhys, born in 1132, who would become the Lord Rhys.[[cite:wiki-gwenllian]]",
  "## 1136",
  "On New Year’s Day 1136 the Welsh of Brycheiniog beat a Norman army in Gower, and all of the south began to rise. Gruffudd ap Rhys rode to Gwynedd to bring Gwenllian’s brothers into the war. While he was away, the Normans of Kidwelly under Maurice de Londres came out to raid. Gwenllian did not wait. She raised an army and led it herself, with two of her sons beside her, against the castle at Kidwelly.[[cite:gerald-itin-kidwelly,wiki-gwenllian]]",
  "> his wife Gwenliana (like the queen of the Amazons, and a second Penthesilea) led an army into these parts | Gerald of Wales, Itinerary through Wales, 1191",
  "The battle went against her. Gwenllian fell on the field north of the town, and her son Morgan fell with her. Her son Maelgwn was taken prisoner. The field has been called Maes Gwenllian ever since, and a spring there, Ffynnon Gwenllian, is said to have risen where she died.[[cite:gerald-itin-kidwelly,wiki-gwenllian]]",
  "## The war she began",
  "Her death did not end the rising. It fired it. That summer her brothers Owain and Cadwaladr came south with the army of Gwynedd, and with her husband Gruffudd they won the great victory of Crug Mawr near Cardigan, in October 1136, and swept the Normans out of Ceredigion. The fight she began was finished by her son, the Lord Rhys, who made Deheubarth the strongest kingdom in Wales.[[cite:wiki-gwenllian,brut-jones]]",
  "Welsh tradition holds that for generations afterwards Welsh soldiers called on her name as they went into battle. She is the only woman in medieval Britain known to have led a Welsh army in war, and she stands with Buddug among the heroines of Wales.[[cite:wiki-gwenllian]]"],
 ["Tywysoges Gwynedd a Deheubarth oedd Gwenllian ferch Gruffudd, ac yn 1136 marchogodd ar flaen byddin yn erbyn y Normaniaid. Hi oedd merch Gruffudd ap Cynan, chwaer Owain Gwynedd, a mam yr Arglwydd Rhys. Syrthiodd mewn brwydr ger Cydweli, a chyneuodd ei marwolaeth dde a gorllewin Cymru gyfan. Cymharodd Gerallt Gymro hi, hanner canrif yn ddiweddarach, â brenhines yr Amasoniaid.",
  "|| Tad | Gruffudd ap Cynan, brenin Gwynedd",
  "|| Mam | Angharad ferch Owain",
  "|| Brodyr | Owain Gwynedd a Chadwaladr",
  "|| Priododd | Gruffudd ap Rhys, tywysog Deheubarth, yn fuan wedi 1116",
  "|| Meibion | Morgan, Maelgwn, Maredudd, Rhys (yr Arglwydd Rhys) ac eraill",
  "|| Syrthiodd | 1136, ym Maes Gwenllian ger Cydweli",
  "## Merch Gwynedd",
  "Magwyd Gwenllian yn llys ei thad Gruffudd ap Cynan, a oedd wedi ennill Gwynedd yn ôl oddi ar y Normaniaid. Tua 1113 daeth tywysog ifanc o’r de i’r gogledd: Gruffudd ap Rhys, mab Rhys ap Tewdwr, brenin olaf Deheubarth, a brawd Nest. Syrthiodd ef a Gwenllian mewn cariad a mynd ymaith gyda’i gilydd, a phriodwyd hwy yn fuan wedi 1116. Rhannodd ei flynyddoedd ym mryniau Ystrad Tywi wrth iddo ymladd i ennill teyrnas ei dad yn ôl, a ganed iddynt lawer o blant, yn eu plith Rhys, a aned yn 1132, a ddaeth yn Arglwydd Rhys.[[cite:wiki-gwenllian]]",
  "## 1136",
  "Ar Ddydd Calan 1136 trechodd Cymry Brycheiniog fyddin Normanaidd yng Ngŵyr, a dechreuodd y de i gyd godi. Marchogodd Gruffudd ap Rhys i Wynedd i dynnu brodyr Gwenllian i’r rhyfel. Tra oedd ef i ffwrdd, daeth Normaniaid Cydweli dan Maurice de Londres allan i anrheithio. Ni arhosodd Gwenllian. Cododd fyddin a’i harwain ei hun, gyda dau o’i meibion wrth ei hochr, yn erbyn y castell yng Nghydweli.[[cite:gerald-itin-kidwelly,wiki-gwenllian]]",
  "> arweiniodd ei wraig Gwenllian (fel brenhines yr Amasoniaid, ac ail Penthesilea) fyddin i’r parthau hyn | Gerallt Gymro, Hanes y Daith trwy Gymru, 1191",
  "Aeth y frwydr yn ei herbyn. Syrthiodd Gwenllian ar y maes i’r gogledd o’r dref, a syrthiodd ei mab Morgan gyda hi. Daliwyd ei mab Maelgwn yn garcharor. Maes Gwenllian yw enw’r maes byth oddi ar hynny, a dywedir i ffynnon yno, Ffynnon Gwenllian, darddu lle bu farw.[[cite:gerald-itin-kidwelly,wiki-gwenllian]]",
  "## Y rhyfel a ddechreuodd",
  "Ni roddodd ei marwolaeth ddiwedd ar y gwrthryfel. Fe’i taniodd. Yr haf hwnnw daeth ei brodyr Owain a Chadwaladr i’r de gyda byddin Gwynedd, a chyda’i gŵr Gruffudd enillasant fuddugoliaeth fawr Crug Mawr ger Aberteifi, ym mis Hydref 1136, ac ysgubo’r Normaniaid allan o Geredigion. Gorffennwyd y frwydr a ddechreuodd hi gan ei mab, yr Arglwydd Rhys, a wnaeth Ddeheubarth yn deyrnas gryfaf Cymru.[[cite:wiki-gwenllian,brut-jones]]",
  "Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, am genedlaethau wedyn galwai milwyr Cymru ar ei henw wrth fynd i’r frwydr. Hi yw’r unig wraig ym Mhrydain yr Oesoedd Canol y gwyddys iddi arwain byddin Gymreig i ryfel, a saif gyda Buddug ymhlith arwresau Cymru.[[cite:wiki-gwenllian]]"],
)

LONG["gwenllian-ferch-llywelyn"] = (
 ["Gwenllian ferch Llywelyn was the only child of Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, the last native Prince of Wales, and of Eleanor de Montfort. She was born a princess of Wales in June 1282. Within a year her mother was dead, her father had fallen at Cilmeri and her uncle Dafydd had been taken. She was carried out of Wales as a baby and kept for the rest of her life in a priory in Lincolnshire, so that no Welsh army could ever rally to her name. Today Wales remembers her as Gwenllian, Princess of Wales.",
  "|| Born | June 1282, at the royal court of Abergwyngregyn",
  "|| Father | Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, Prince of Wales",
  "|| Mother | Eleanor de Montfort, Princess of Wales",
  "|| Taken to | the Gilbertine priory of Sempringham, Lincolnshire, 1283",
  "|| Died | 7 June 1337, at Sempringham, aged nearly fifty-five",
  "|| Remembered | Carnedd Gwenllian in the Carneddau, named for her in 2009",
  "## Born at Abergwyngregyn",
  "Llywelyn had waited many years for an heir. He married Eleanor de Montfort at Worcester in 1278, and in June 1282, in the middle of the last war for Welsh freedom, Eleanor gave birth to a daughter at the court of Abergwyngregyn, beneath the mountains of Eryri. Eleanor died on 19 June, a few days after the birth. On 11 December that year Llywelyn fell at Cilmeri, and the six-month-old Gwenllian was the heir of the princes of Wales.[[cite:wiki-gwenllian-wales]]",
  "## Sempringham",
  "Her uncle Dafydd ap Gruffudd fought on as Prince of Wales until he was taken in June 1283. Then Edward I gathered up the children of the royal house of Gwynedd. Dafydd’s two sons were shut in Bristol Castle. Dafydd’s daughters and Gwenllian were sent to remote priories in Lincolnshire, far from any Welshman. Gwenllian was placed with the nuns of the Gilbertine priory at Sempringham, among the fens, and there she stayed for fifty-four years.[[cite:wiki-gwenllian-wales]]",
  "The nuns wrote her name as Wencilian, and she signed herself Wentliane. Edward III granted her a pension of twenty pounds a year, paid to the priory for her food and clothing. She died on 7 June 1337, a few days before her fifty-fifth birthday, and the priory’s chronicler recorded her death.[[cite:wiki-gwenllian-wales]]",
  "## Remembered",
  "For centuries she was almost forgotten, but in our own time Wales has given her back her name. A memorial of Welsh granite stands near the church of St Andrew at Sempringham. A plaque to “the Princess Gwenllian” was set below the summit of Yr Wyddfa in 2008. On 26 September 2009 the mountain Carnedd Uchaf in the Carneddau, above her birthplace at Abergwyngregyn, was renamed Carnedd Gwenllian in her memory. The poets T. James Jones and Mererid Hopwood have written of her.[[cite:wiki-gwenllian-wales]]"],
 ["Gwenllian ferch Llywelyn oedd unig blentyn Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, Tywysog Cymru olaf o’n gwaed ein hunain, ac Eleanor de Montfort. Fe’i ganed yn dywysoges Cymru ym mis Mehefin 1282. O fewn blwyddyn roedd ei mam yn farw, ei thad wedi syrthio yng Nghilmeri a’i hewythr Dafydd wedi’i ddal. Cariwyd hi allan o Gymru’n faban a’i chadw am weddill ei hoes mewn priordy yn swydd Lincoln, fel na allai’r un fyddin Gymreig fyth ymgasglu dan ei henw. Heddiw mae Cymru’n ei chofio fel Gwenllian, Tywysoges Cymru.",
  "|| Ganed | Mehefin 1282, yn llys brenhinol Abergwyngregyn",
  "|| Tad | Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, Tywysog Cymru",
  "|| Mam | Eleanor de Montfort, Tywysoges Cymru",
  "|| Ei chludo i | briordy Gilbertaidd Sempringham, swydd Lincoln, 1283",
  "|| Bu farw | 7 Mehefin 1337, yn Sempringham, bron yn bum deg pump oed",
  "|| Ei chofio | Carnedd Gwenllian yn y Carneddau, a enwyd ar ei hôl yn 2009",
  "## Ei geni yn Abergwyngregyn",
  "Roedd Llywelyn wedi aros blynyddoedd lawer am etifedd. Priododd Eleanor de Montfort yng Nghaerwrangon yn 1278, ac ym mis Mehefin 1282, yng nghanol y rhyfel olaf dros ryddid Cymru, ganwyd merch i Eleanor yn llys Abergwyngregyn, dan fynyddoedd Eryri. Bu farw Eleanor ar 19 Mehefin, ychydig ddyddiau wedi’r enedigaeth. Ar 11 Rhagfyr y flwyddyn honno syrthiodd Llywelyn yng Nghilmeri, a Gwenllian, yn chwe mis oed, oedd etifedd tywysogion Cymru.[[cite:wiki-gwenllian-wales]]",
  "## Sempringham",
  "Ymladdodd ei hewythr Dafydd ap Gruffudd ymlaen yn Dywysog Cymru nes ei ddal ym mis Mehefin 1283. Yna casglodd Edward I blant tŷ brenhinol Gwynedd ynghyd. Caewyd dau fab Dafydd yng Nghastell Bryste. Anfonwyd merched Dafydd a Gwenllian i briordai anghysbell yn swydd Lincoln, ymhell o bob Cymro. Rhoddwyd Gwenllian gyda lleianod priordy Gilbertaidd Sempringham, ymhlith y corsydd, ac yno y bu am bum deg pedair blynedd.[[cite:wiki-gwenllian-wales]]",
  "Ysgrifennai’r lleianod ei henw fel Wencilian, a’i lofnodi fel Wentliane a wnâi hi. Rhoddodd Edward III bensiwn o ugain punt y flwyddyn iddi, a delid i’r priordy am ei bwyd a’i dillad. Bu farw ar 7 Mehefin 1337, ychydig ddyddiau cyn ei phen-blwydd yn bum deg pump, a chofnododd croniclydd y priordy ei marwolaeth.[[cite:wiki-gwenllian-wales]]",
  "## Ei chofio",
  "Am ganrifoedd bu bron yn angof, ond yn ein hoes ni mae Cymru wedi rhoi ei henw yn ôl iddi. Saif cofeb o wenithfaen Cymreig ger eglwys Sant Andreas yn Sempringham. Gosodwyd plac i “y Dywysoges Gwenllian” islaw copa’r Wyddfa yn 2008. Ar 26 Medi 2009 ailenwyd y mynydd Carnedd Uchaf yn y Carneddau, uwchben ei man geni yn Abergwyngregyn, yn Garnedd Gwenllian er cof amdani. Mae’r beirdd T. James Jones a Mererid Hopwood wedi canu amdani.[[cite:wiki-gwenllian-wales]]"],
)

LONG["owain-lawgoch"] = (
 ["Owain Lawgoch, Owain of the Red Hand, was the last heir of the royal house of Gwynedd in the male line, the great-nephew of Llywelyn ap Gruffudd and the great-great-grandson of Llywelyn Fawr. The French called him Yvain de Galles, Owain of Wales, and counted him among the finest captains of his age. In 1372 he proclaimed his right to Wales and sailed with a French fleet to win it, and the English crown feared him so much that in the end it paid a man to kill him.",
  "|| Born | about 1330, at Tatsfield in Surrey",
  "|| Father | Thomas ap Rhodri, grandson of Gruffudd ap Llywelyn Fawr",
  "|| Known in France as | Yvain de Galles",
  "|| Claimed Wales | Paris, 10 May 1372",
  "|| Victories | Soubise, 1372; the war in Poitou and Saintonge",
  "|| Died | July 1378, at the siege of Mortagne-sur-Gironde",
  "|| Buried | the church of Saint-Léger, near Mortagne",
  "## The heir of the Llywelyns",
  "Owain was born about 1330 on his father’s English estate at Tatsfield in Surrey. His grandfather Rhodri was the youngest brother of Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, and after 1283 Owain’s line was the last of the house of Gwynedd. He went to France while still young and entered the service of King Philip VI, and he spent almost all the rest of his life abroad as a leader of soldiers in France, Lombardy, Brittany, Alsace and Switzerland. He knew well whose heir he was, and he spoke of it at the French court.[[cite:dwb-owain-lawgoch,wiki-owain-lawgoch]]",
  "In 1365, in a time of truce, he came to England to claim his father’s lands, and in March 1366 he went back to France. When war broke out again in 1369 he fought for the king of France, and the English took everything he had in England and Wales.[[cite:dwb-owain-lawgoch]]",
  "## 1372",
  "King Charles V of France saw what Owain’s name could do in Wales. On 10 May 1372, in Paris, Owain declared his claim to the land of his fathers, and Charles lent him money and a fleet. He sailed from Harfleur with a company of Welshmen and French soldiers, and on the way he landed on Guernsey and fought the English there, deeds the islanders sang of for centuries. Then the king called him away to the war in the west. That summer, at Soubise in Saintonge, Owain broke an English and Gascon army and took prisoner two of its leaders, Sir Thomas Percy and the Captal de Buch, one of the most famous soldiers in Europe.[[cite:dwb-owain-lawgoch,wiki-owain-lawgoch]]",
  "> which is and should be mine by right of succession | Owain Lawgoch, declaration at Paris, 1372",
  "In Wales the English were afraid. As early as December 1369 they were preparing for an invasion, and in 1370 a man of Ynys Môn was condemned for dealings with “Owen Lawgoch”. The poets of Wales began to sing of a deliverer of that name.[[cite:dwb-owain-lawgoch]]",
  "## Mortagne",
  "In 1375 Owain led men for Enguerrand de Coucy in his war in Switzerland, and through the 1370s he fought on in Poitou and Saintonge. In 1377 there was word of a new expedition to Wales with ships from Castile. The English crown sent a Scot named John Lamb to France. Lamb won Owain’s trust and became his squire, and in July 1378, while Owain was besieging the castle of Mortagne-sur-Gironde, Lamb stabbed him. On 4 December 1378 the English Exchequer paid John Lamb twenty pounds for the killing of “Owynn de Gales”. Owain was buried in the church of Saint-Léger nearby, and he was mourned by the many who had fought beside him.[[cite:dwb-owain-lawgoch,wiki-owain-lawgoch]]",
  "## The sleeper in the cave",
  "Wales did not forget him. The poets kept his name in their prophecies, and twenty years later Owain Glyndŵr took up the hope of the mab darogan, the son of destiny, that Owain had carried. It is told in Ceredigion and Carmarthenshire that Owain Lawgoch sleeps with his men in a cave, and that when he wakes he will be king of the Britons. The lake at Aberllefenni was once called Llyn Owain Lawgoch. On 15 August 2003 a memorial to him was unveiled at Mortagne-sur-Gironde.[[cite:wiki-owain-lawgoch,dwb-owain-lawgoch]]"],
 ["Owain Lawgoch oedd etifedd olaf tŷ brenhinol Gwynedd yn llinach y gwrywod, ŵyr i frawd Llywelyn ap Gruffudd a gor-orŵyr i Lywelyn Fawr. Galwai’r Ffrancwyr ef yn Yvain de Galles, Owain o Gymru, a’i gyfrif ymhlith capteiniaid gorau ei oes. Yn 1372 cyhoeddodd ei hawl i Gymru a hwylio gyda llynges Ffrengig i’w hennill, ac ofnai coron Lloegr ef gymaint nes iddi, yn y diwedd, dalu dyn i’w ladd.",
  "|| Ganed | tua 1330, yn Tatsfield yn Surrey",
  "|| Tad | Tomas ap Rhodri, ŵyr i Ruffudd ap Llywelyn Fawr",
  "|| Ei enw yn Ffrainc | Yvain de Galles",
  "|| Hawliodd Gymru | Paris, 10 Mai 1372",
  "|| Buddugoliaethau | Soubise, 1372; y rhyfel yn Poitou a Saintonge",
  "|| Bu farw | Gorffennaf 1378, yng ngwarchae Mortagne-sur-Gironde",
  "|| Claddwyd | eglwys Saint-Léger, ger Mortagne",
  "## Etifedd y Llywelyniaid",
  "Ganed Owain tua 1330 ar ystad Seisnig ei dad yn Tatsfield yn Surrey. Ei daid Rhodri oedd brawd ieuengaf Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, ac wedi 1283 llinach Owain oedd yr olaf o dŷ Gwynedd. Aeth i Ffrainc yn ifanc ac i wasanaeth y Brenin Philippe VI, a threuliodd bron weddill ei oes dramor yn arwain milwyr yn Ffrainc, Lombardia, Llydaw, Alsace a’r Swistir. Gwyddai’n dda etifedd pwy ydoedd, a soniai am hynny yn llys Ffrainc.[[cite:dwb-owain-lawgoch,wiki-owain-lawgoch]]",
  "Yn 1365, mewn cyfnod o gadoediad, daeth i Loegr i hawlio tiroedd ei dad, ac ym mis Mawrth 1366 aeth yn ôl i Ffrainc. Pan dorrodd rhyfel allan eto yn 1369 ymladdodd dros frenin Ffrainc, a chymerodd y Saeson bopeth a oedd ganddo yn Lloegr a Chymru.[[cite:dwb-owain-lawgoch]]",
  "## 1372",
  "Gwelodd y Brenin Charles V o Ffrainc beth allai enw Owain ei wneud yng Nghymru. Ar 10 Mai 1372, ym Mharis, cyhoeddodd Owain ei hawl i wlad ei dadau, a rhoddodd Charles fenthyg arian a llynges iddo. Hwyliodd o Harfleur gyda chwmni o Gymry a milwyr Ffrengig, ac ar y ffordd glaniodd ar Ynys y Garn ac ymladd y Saeson yno, gwrhydri y canodd yr ynyswyr amdano am ganrifoedd. Yna galwodd y brenin ef ymaith i’r rhyfel yn y gorllewin. Yr haf hwnnw, yn Soubise yn Saintonge, drylliodd Owain fyddin o Saeson a Gasgwyniaid a chymryd dau o’i harweinwyr yn garcharorion, Syr Thomas Percy a’r Captal de Buch, un o filwyr enwocaf Ewrop.[[cite:dwb-owain-lawgoch,wiki-owain-lawgoch]]",
  "> sydd ac a ddylai fod yn eiddo i mi drwy hawl olyniaeth | Owain Lawgoch, datganiad ym Mharis, 1372",
  "Yng Nghymru roedd ofn ar y Saeson. Mor gynnar â Rhagfyr 1369 roeddent yn paratoi am oresgyniad, ac yn 1370 condemniwyd gŵr o Ynys Môn am ymwneud ag “Owen Lawgoch”. Dechreuodd beirdd Cymru ganu am waredwr o’r enw hwnnw.[[cite:dwb-owain-lawgoch]]",
  "## Mortagne",
  "Yn 1375 arweiniodd Owain wŷr dros Enguerrand de Coucy yn ei ryfel yn y Swistir, a thrwy’r 1370au ymladdodd ymlaen yn Poitou a Saintonge. Yn 1377 daeth sôn am ymgyrch newydd i Gymru gyda llongau o Gastîl. Anfonodd coron Lloegr Albanwr o’r enw John Lamb i Ffrainc. Enillodd Lamb ymddiriedaeth Owain a dod yn ysgwïer iddo, ac ym mis Gorffennaf 1378, tra oedd Owain yn gwarchae ar gastell Mortagne-sur-Gironde, trywanodd Lamb ef. Ar 4 Rhagfyr 1378 talodd Trysorlys Lloegr ugain punt i John Lamb am ladd “Owynn de Gales”. Claddwyd Owain yn eglwys Saint-Léger gerllaw, a galarodd y llu a fu’n ymladd wrth ei ochr amdano.[[cite:dwb-owain-lawgoch,wiki-owain-lawgoch]]",
  "## Y cysgadur yn yr ogof",
  "Nid anghofiodd Cymru ef. Cadwodd y beirdd ei enw yn eu daroganau, ac ugain mlynedd yn ddiweddarach cododd Owain Glyndŵr obaith y mab darogan a gariodd Owain. Dywedir yng Ngheredigion a sir Gaerfyrddin fod Owain Lawgoch yn cysgu gyda’i wŷr mewn ogof, ac y bydd, pan ddeffry, yn frenin y Brytaniaid. Llyn Owain Lawgoch oedd hen enw’r llyn yn Aberllefenni. Ar 15 Awst 2003 dadorchuddiwyd cofeb iddo yn Mortagne-sur-Gironde.[[cite:wiki-owain-lawgoch,dwb-owain-lawgoch]]"],
)

